WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.410 --> 00:00:02.140
<v Examiner>Three marks.

2
00:00:02.140 --> 00:00:07.191
<v Examiner>Describe morphine's class, and identify two adverse effects the nurse monitors for.

3
00:00:07.641 --> 00:00:10.969
Morphine is an opioid analgesic, for severe pain.

4
00:00:10.969 --> 00:00:13.686
It's an agonist at the opioid receptors.

5
00:00:13.686 --> 00:00:17.218
Legally, it's a Class B controlled drug: prescribed,

6
00:00:17.218 --> 00:00:20.207
but double-locked and checked by two nurses.

7
00:00:20.657 --> 00:00:21.723
Adverse effects.

8
00:00:21.723 --> 00:00:24.257
The big one is respiratory depression.

9
00:00:24.257 --> 00:00:30.123
In her overview deck, a patient on a morphine P C A whose breathing slows is her example

10
00:00:30.123 --> 00:00:31.323
of a toxic effect.

11
00:00:31.323 --> 00:00:35.457
So you monitor the respiratory rate and the level of sedation.

12
00:00:35.457 --> 00:00:40.390
The others: nausea, constipation, low blood pressure, drowsiness and itch.

13
00:00:40.840 --> 00:00:43.956
Two more facts that turn up in the multi-choice.

14
00:00:43.956 --> 00:00:48.696
The antidote is naloxone, an opioid antagonist that reverses an overdose.

15
00:00:48.696 --> 00:00:54.344
And morphine users develop tolerance quickly: the same dose stops controlling the pain,

16
00:00:54.344 --> 00:00:56.811
so they need more for the same effect.

17
00:00:56.811 --> 00:01:01.811
Of morphine's routes, by mouth gives the slowest relief, and I V the fastest.

18
00:01:02.261 --> 00:01:07.896
Morphine: an opioid analgesic for severe pain, an agonist, Class B controlled.

19
00:01:07.896 --> 00:01:13.603
Toxic effect: respiratory depression, so monitor respiratory rate and sedation.

20
00:01:13.603 --> 00:01:16.926
Also nausea, constipation, low blood pressure.

21
00:01:16.926 --> 00:01:19.382
Antidote: naloxone, an antagonist.

22
00:01:19.382 --> 00:01:21.333
Tolerance develops quickly.
